ild_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.7.2 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/bd5868075aeeec220c745ea9f5716cfc14a96d1d/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Root cause: `to_cents` truncates float error \u2014 `19.99 * 100 == 1998.9999\u2026` \u2192 `int()` gives 1998. Same for 0.29 and 4.35.\npytest isn't installed, so I'll run the test functions directly.\nFixed. The bug was in `ledger/currency.py`: `to_cents` used `int(amount * 100)`. Floats can't store most decimal prices exactly, so `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.9999\u2026`, and `int()` just cuts off the fraction, losing a cent (19.99 \u2192 1998, 0.29 \u2192 28, 4.35 \u2192 434). Every total built on those prices was therefore wrong.\n\nThe fix:\n```python\nreturn int(round(amount * 100))\n```\n\npytest isn't installed here and pip install was refused, so I couldn't run the suite normally. Instead I imported and called every `test_*` function in `tests/test_totals.py` and `tests/test_report.py` directly, and all 5 pass.\n\n`round()` brings values that are a hair off back to the intended cent. It doesn't handle prices with more than two decimal places in any special way: Python rounds an exact half to the even number, and a float near a half can land either way. That was never defined before either, since the old code truncated those. If prices with sub-cent precision are possible, the real fix is to pass prices in as `Decimal` or strings, not floats. That's a bigger change and I did